course: chemistry
lesson: 37
title: Olympiad: everyday chemistry detectives ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Out of the kitchen and into the house, the garden and the street: these puzzles make you a chemistry detective, reading clues on a bike, a spoon, a candle or a kettle.
explain: Most clues point to a few big ideas from the course: what a change needs (rust needs air and water, fire needs fuel, heat and oxygen), what a thing is made of, and whether a change can be undone.
explain: Picture three nails in three jars: one in dry air, one in boiled water under a layer of oil, one in ordinary tap water. A week later only one is properly rusty, and a detective knows which before looking.
explain: People talk big and small: "my bike's a total pile of rust" may mean three orange spots, while "the kettle's got a bit of fur" may mean a crust as thick as a coin - a detective checks the real size.
explain: These puzzles are for thinking, not trying: fire, cleaning products and batteries are a grown-up's job.
fact: Iron rusts only when it has both oxygen and water; salt from sea spray or gritted roads makes it rust faster.
fact: Paint stops rust until it is scratched; galvanised steel has a zinc coat that protects the iron even at a scratch.
fact: Tetanus is caused by a germ in soil and dirt, not by rust; a deep cut from any dirty object can let it in, and vaccination protects you.
fact: Silver turns black from sulphur gases, which eggs give off; gold hardly ever tarnishes, and copper slowly grows a green coat called patina.
fact: Fire needs fuel, heat and oxygen; a candle under a jar goes out when the oxygen runs low, so a bigger jar keeps it burning longer.
fact: In a candle it is the wax gas that burns, so a freshly blown-out candle can relight through its smoke; water must never go on burning oil.
fact: Only iron, nickel, cobalt and steel are pulled by a magnet; British pennies and 2p coins made since 1992 are steel with a copper coat, so they stick.
fact: In chalk and limestone areas tap water is hard, and heating it leaves white limescale; limescale is mostly calcium carbonate, like chalk, so it fizzes with acid such as vinegar.
fact: Soap and detergent do the cleaning, not the foam; washing-up liquid must never go in a dishwasher, because its foam would overflow.
fact: Hydrangeas turn blue in acidic soil and stay pink in alkaline soil.
fact: Chromatography splits an ink into its colours, which travel with water at different speeds; it can show which pen wrote a note.
fact: Air is about 78 parts nitrogen, 21 parts oxygen and 1 part other gases in 100; nitrogen hardly reacts, so crisp packets are filled with it.
fact: Helium atoms slip slowly through rubber, so a helium balloon shrinks in a few days; cold air in a tyre shrinks too, so tyre pressure drops on frosty mornings.
fact: The best order for less waste is reduce, then reuse, then recycle; glass and aluminium can be recycled again and again, paper only a few times, and batteries go in special collection boxes.
fact: Words from round the world: "dreich" (Scotland) means grey and drizzly, "lekker" (South Africa) means great, "arvo" (Australia) means afternoon, and "y'all" (southern US) means you all.
exercise: Nail A sits in dry air with a drying powder, nail B in boiled water under a layer of oil, nail C half in ordinary tap water. Which rusts most? | C, because it has both air and water | A, as it has the most air around it | B, as it has the most water around it | All the same, as all three are iron
exercise: Which is the odd one out: a nail rusting, a spoon tarnishing, candle wax melting, wood burning? | Melting wax: no new substance is made | Rusting nail: the only one that needs oxygen | Burning wood: the only chemical change | Tarnishing spoon: the only metal one
exercise: The same model of car rusts faster in a seaside town than in a dry town far inland. Which two clues together explain it? | Sea air is damp and carries salt | Sea air has more oxygen in it | Waves knock the paint off at night | Sea sand blows in and turns to rust
exercise: A painted steel gate and a galvanised steel bin both get a deep scratch. Which one starts rusting at the scratch? | The painted gate | The galvanised bin, as zinc rusts at once | Both at exactly the same moment | Neither, as a scratch can never rust
exercise: Two candles are lit together and covered, one by a small jam jar and one by a big pickle jar. Which goes out first? | The jam-jar candle, as it has less oxygen | The pickle-jar candle, as big jars trap smoke | Both together, as candles burn a fixed time | Neither, as candles make their own air
exercise: A grown-up blows out a candle and holds a lit match in the rising smoke; the flame jumps down and relights the wick. What does this show? | It is the wax gas that does the burning | The string is the part that really burns | Smoke is made mostly of pure oxygen | The match makes the wax melt into water
exercise: Which of these would a magnet pick up? | A British 2p coin from 2010 | An aluminium drinks can | A gold wedding ring | A short length of copper water pipe
exercise: A kettle in a chalky part of Kent is crusted white inside, but the same kettle in a soft-water town stays clean. Why? | Kent's water carries dissolved chalk that comes out when heated | The other town's kettles are made of better steel | Kent's water is salty, so salt crystals grow | The crust is soap left over from washing up
exercise: Which kitchen liquid would help loosen that white crust, and what clue would you see? | Vinegar, and it would fizz a little | Washing-up liquid, and it would foam up | Cooking oil, and it would float on top | Milk, and it would go lumpy
exercise: Nan's hydrangea was pink last year. This year she dug lots of acidic compost into the soil around it. What colour might it turn? | Blue | Bright yellow | Pink, as the colour never changes | Dark red
exercise: Pen 1's ink splits into blue and yellow, pen 2's into blue and red, and pen 3's stays black. The ink from a secret note splits into blue and red. Which pen wrote it? | Pen 2 | Pen 1 | Pen 3 | None of the three
exercise: Which is the best first step to cut the waste from drinks bottles? | Use one refillable bottle | Recycle every bottle you buy | Burn the bottles for heat | Bury them deep in landfill
exercise: Which pair can be recycled again and again without wearing out? | Glass jars and drinks cans | Newspapers and cardboard | Greasy pizza boxes | Crisp packets and wrappers
exercise: Why are crisp packets filled with nitrogen rather than ordinary air? | Nitrogen hardly reacts, so the crisps stay fresh | Nitrogen makes the crisps taste saltier | Nitrogen is so light that the packet floats | Ordinary air turns crisps soggy within seconds
exercise: In 100 parts of air, 78 are nitrogen and 21 are oxygen. About how many parts are left for all the other gases? | About 1 | About 21 | About 22 | About 10
exercise: Which change would a detective call a chemical change? | A copper roof turning green | A puddle drying in the sun | Butter going soft on a warm day | A balloon shrinking in the cold
test: «me bike's a total pile of rust, can't even ride it» - you look and find three small orange spots on the chain guard. What's really going on? | He's exaggerating: a few spots, best cleaned and painted | It has truly turned to rust from end to end | The bike is brand new and has no rust at all | The orange is paint that rubbed off a gate
test: «the kettle's got a bit of fur in it» says Dad. Inside there is a white crust as thick as a coin. What is true? | He's playing it down: there's a lot of limescale | He's exaggerating: there's hardly any limescale in there | It is real fur shed by the cat | It is mould that grew in the hot water
test: Ruby reckons "rust gives you tetanus, so rusty stuff is poison." Which answer puts her right? | Tetanus is a germ in dirt; a deep cut from anything dirty can let it in | She's right; the rust itself is the germ | Rust is safe; only shiny metal causes it | Tetanus floats in the air of rusty sheds
test: A Scottish gran says: «it's a dreich day, and look, me silver egg spoons have gone black. it's the rain, aye?» What is the better explanation? | No, sulphur gas from the eggs tarnished them | Aye, rain turns silver black overnight | Aye, grey weather makes silver go dark | No, the damp air is making the silver rust like iron
test: A cartoon shows a cook throwing a bucket of water onto a burning chip pan, and the fire goes out at once. What is wrong? | Water on burning oil throws it out in a fireball | Nothing, water puts out every kind of fire | The water would freeze the oil solid at once | Nothing, but a bigger bucket would be better
test: «just chuck the dead batteries in the bin innit, they're empty anyway» What is the honest reply? | No: they belong in collection boxes, as they can leak and start fires | Fine, dead batteries have nothing left inside them | Fine, as long as you put them in the bin when it rains | No, old batteries should go on the garden bonfire
test: In Cape Town, Sipho says: "Squeeze a half-empty soda bottle before you close it and it stays fizzier, lekker trick!" Is he right? | No, squeezing doesn't keep it fizzier | Yes, the squeeze pushes the gas back into the drink | Yes, but it works only with diet drinks | No, the squeeze makes the bottle explode
test: «y'all, the tyre's flat as a pancake this frosty morning, it was fine yesterday arvo» The tyre looks only a little soft. What is the best detective guess? | The cold air inside shrank, so the pressure dropped a bit | The frost froze the rubber solid and it cracked | Tyres get much heavier in cold weather | The air inside turned into water
test: «washing-up liquid in the dishwasher, what's the worst that could happen lol» | Foam pours out all over the kitchen floor | The plates come out extra shiny and clean | Nothing at all, because soap is just soap | The dishwasher freezes up with ice inside
test: «this bubble bath must be cleaning me heaps, look at all the foam» What is true? | The soap cleans, foam or no foam | Yes, more foam always means cleaner skin | No, bubbles leave you dirtier than before | Yes, but only the biggest bubbles clean
test: A helium balloon from Saturday is small and wrinkly by Wednesday, with no hole. A little kid says it is "sad". What is really happening? | Helium slips out through the rubber | The helium has slowly turned into air | The balloon got heavier and sank in the cold | The sunlight dried the balloon out
exam: Identify the conditions under which an iron nail will rust most rapidly. | In salty water that is open to the air | In a sealed jar of perfectly dry air | In boiled water beneath a layer of oil | In a vacuum with no water present
exam: Determine which of the following objects would be attracted by a magnet. | A steel paperclip | An aluminium drinks can | A length of copper wire | A pure gold coin
exam: A candle placed beneath an inverted jar is extinguished. Ascertain the reason. | The oxygen within the jar has largely been used up | The jar has cooled the wax so that it cannot melt | The wick has been dissolved by water vapour | The carbon dioxide inside has caught fire
exam: State which ordering of waste practices is recommended. | Reduce, then reuse, then recycle | Recycle, then reduce, then reuse | Reuse, then recycle, then reduce | Landfill, then recycle, then reduce
